1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:05,967
When describing a game that does not exist yet, can a familiar title make everyone

2
00:00:05,967 --> 00:00:07,080
picture the same thing?

3
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:11,522
We will put Pepper Grinder’s movement into verbs, then distinguish conditions

4
00:00:11,522 --> 00:00:15,440
through The Plucky Squire’s actions across illustrated surfaces and a desk.

5
00:00:15,640 --> 00:00:19,604
By the end, you can write the goal, actions and conditions in three sentences and

6
00:00:19,604 --> 00:00:22,100
use a listener’s questions to find what is missing.

7
00:00:22,360 --> 00:00:25,420
Let us begin by watching the character drill out of yellow terrain.

8
00:00:25,450 --> 00:00:30,401
The character changes direction inside yellow terrain, emerges, and moves through

9
00:00:30,401 --> 00:00:30,890
the air.

10
00:00:31,130 --> 00:00:33,570
Notice the brambles between routes and the separated pieces of terrain.

11
00:00:33,997 --> 00:00:37,557
Hanging from a hook and launching from a cannon are different kinds of movement.

12
00:00:37,817 --> 00:00:40,977
Calling all of it running can hide what the character actually does.

13
00:00:41,217 --> 00:00:46,406
In the dark cave, the character curves along a low passage and emerges into the air

14
00:00:46,406 --> 00:00:46,690
again.

15
00:00:46,950 --> 00:00:49,910
Start by writing the action verbs and the spaces where those actions happen.

16
00:00:50,190 --> 00:00:52,030
Here we are describing visible movement.

17
00:00:52,270 --> 00:00:56,067
This short demonstration does not establish the exact buttons or the rules for every

18
00:00:56,067 --> 00:00:56,790
kind of terrain.

19
00:00:57,350 --> 00:01:01,247
If a new concept is introduced only as being like a familiar game, listeners fill in

20
00:01:01,247 --> 00:01:03,010
the gaps with the parts they remember.

21
00:01:03,110 --> 00:01:05,070
Imagine two hypothetical listeners.

22
00:01:05,290 --> 00:01:10,082
One might picture movement over obstacles, while the other first pictures fighting

23
00:01:10,082 --> 00:01:10,550
enemies.

24
00:01:10,790 --> 00:01:14,050
This is an explanation exercise, not a result from a user study.

25
00:01:14,390 --> 00:01:17,911
We are avoiding the assumption that hearing the same title means picturing the same

26
00:01:17,911 --> 00:01:18,250
actions.

27
00:01:18,530 --> 00:01:21,490
If you use a comparison, say which part you mean.

28
00:01:21,630 --> 00:01:25,660
That helps prevent the listener from supplying the rest of your unbuilt game’s

29
00:01:25,660 --> 00:01:25,970
rules.

30
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:27,900
Now watch The Plucky Squire.

31
00:01:28,100 --> 00:01:32,020
Begin with walking on an illustrated book surface and crossing a gap between books.

32
00:01:32,340 --> 00:01:36,358
Passing through a green entry point brings the character onto the three-dimensional

33
00:01:36,358 --> 00:01:36,600
desk.

34
00:01:36,860 --> 00:01:40,283
Another shot shows movement between the desk and a pictured surface with printed

35
00:01:40,283 --> 00:01:40,540
doors.

36
00:01:43,870 --> 00:01:47,220
There is also a sequence of approaching an entry point on a cup and entering its

37
00:01:47,220 --> 00:01:47,890
printed surface.

38
00:01:48,190 --> 00:01:51,150
Separately, watch the character leave that surface and appear on the desk.

39
00:01:54,010 --> 00:01:57,064
Saying only that the game mixes two and three dimensions leaves out where the

40
00:01:57,064 --> 00:01:58,650
character enters and what happens there.

41
00:01:58,990 --> 00:02:01,730
Describe the surface and the entry point together.

42
00:02:02,935 --> 00:02:06,075
This is a desk inside the game, rather than a live-action advertisement outside it.

43
00:02:06,355 --> 00:02:10,075
The explanation is based on actions visibly performed in that virtual space.

44
00:02:11,417 --> 00:02:15,113
When writing the explanation, connect the space, the action verb and the visible

45
00:02:15,113 --> 00:02:15,437
change:

46
00:02:15,777 --> 00:02:20,437
approaching an entry point on the desk and entering a printed surface, for example.

47
00:02:20,677 --> 00:02:24,892
Adding this sentence next to a phrase like enjoyable movement gives the listener a

48
00:02:24,892 --> 00:02:26,537
more concrete action to picture.

49
00:02:26,837 --> 00:02:29,717
A title or genre can provide context.

50
00:02:29,957 --> 00:02:33,637
Use your own sentence to convey the central action of the new concept.

51
00:02:33,667 --> 00:02:36,867
Return to Pepper’s waterside and routes above lava.

52
00:02:37,207 --> 00:02:41,540
A walkable bridge, curved terrain and an elastic purple surface show different

53
00:02:41,540 --> 00:02:43,375
spaces and movements.

54
00:02:43,535 --> 00:02:47,126
Specifying where the movement happens, such as a sloping route or an ice surface

55
00:02:47,126 --> 00:02:49,775
beside wooden braces, narrows what your explanation covers.

56
00:02:50,055 --> 00:02:53,731
During Plucky’s rocket movement, the character rises among spool tops and card

57
00:02:53,731 --> 00:02:56,713
ledges, strikes enemies, and approaches an entry point on a wall.

58
00:02:57,013 --> 00:03:01,389
Also watch the character descend into a mug, pass a glowing object, and rise again.

59
00:03:01,389 --> 00:03:04,459
You can describe ascent, descent and the spaces reached separately.

60
00:03:05,033 --> 00:03:09,233
These observations do not establish unlimited flight or a fuel-refill rule.

61
00:03:09,693 --> 00:03:12,073
Keep visible conditions separate from questions that need further checking.

62
00:03:14,283 --> 00:03:19,703
For a new concept, decide how entry points will be indicated and when an action can

63
00:03:19,703 --> 00:03:20,943
be performed again.

64
00:03:21,263 --> 00:03:24,263
Write these questions beside the observed facts, in a separate place.

65
00:03:24,503 --> 00:03:27,683
A time limit or a cost is a design question we are proposing here.

66
00:03:28,003 --> 00:03:32,391
Filling a gap with a remembered assumption about a reference game can make an

67
00:03:32,391 --> 00:03:34,043
undecided rule sound settled.

68
00:03:34,423 --> 00:03:38,123
Explain the conditions you have established, and leave undecided ones as questions.

69
00:03:38,343 --> 00:03:41,223
That also shows the listener where their input is needed.

70
00:03:41,250 --> 00:03:44,773
In the mine sequence, the character approaches an entry point on the illustrated

71
00:03:44,773 --> 00:03:46,270
surface and emerges onto the desk.

72
00:03:46,570 --> 00:03:50,050
Distinguish the character walking from the book itself tilting.

73
00:03:50,350 --> 00:03:55,986
When the book tilts left, the yellow key moves left; in another portion, it moves

74
00:03:55,986 --> 00:03:56,401
right.

75
00:03:56,701 --> 00:04:00,523
Record the visible directions, but this demonstration does not establish the exact

76
00:04:00,523 --> 00:04:02,061
input or a rule for every object.

77
00:04:02,341 --> 00:04:07,875
For your new concept, spell out what moves and what changes.

78
00:04:08,155 --> 00:04:11,735
That sentence exposes conditions you may have expected a familiar title to supply.

79
00:04:11,767 --> 00:04:15,667
On the desk, the character approaches and attacks enemies and throws an object.

80
00:04:16,007 --> 00:04:19,592
Describing movement through the space is not the same as describing how enemies are

81
00:04:19,592 --> 00:04:20,067
confronted.

82
00:04:23,785 --> 00:04:26,505
Inside the blue printed surface, the character jumps and strikes enemies.

83
00:04:26,825 --> 00:04:29,665
The surface on which the action takes place has changed from the desk.

84
00:04:30,275 --> 00:04:34,605
Approaching an entry point on a large cylinder and entering its picture is another

85
00:04:34,605 --> 00:04:34,975
action.

86
00:04:35,195 --> 00:04:39,310
Distinguish entry from combat that follows it.

87
00:04:39,610 --> 00:04:43,642
Adding a sentence for what happens in each space reveals differences hidden by the

88
00:04:43,642 --> 00:04:44,970
phrase changing dimensions.

89
00:04:45,133 --> 00:04:47,893
Now write a new concept in three sentences.

90
00:04:48,253 --> 00:04:52,735
The first gives the player’s goal; the second gives the repeated action and its

91
00:04:52,735 --> 00:04:53,813
central conditions.

92
00:04:54,233 --> 00:04:58,133
The third explains how that action connects to another.

93
00:04:58,573 --> 00:05:01,353
The following is our explanation exercise using the scenes we have watched.

94
00:05:02,013 --> 00:05:03,993
The goal is to reach the next platform.

95
00:05:04,253 --> 00:05:07,073
The action is drilling along a route inside soft terrain.

96
00:05:07,353 --> 00:05:10,530
Emerging from that terrain connects the action to moving through the air toward

97
00:05:10,530 --> 00:05:11,093
another space.

98
00:05:11,193 --> 00:05:14,786
These sentences do not reproduce the developer’s design document or establish the

99
00:05:14,786 --> 00:05:15,673
game’s overall goal.

100
00:05:15,933 --> 00:05:19,310
For your own concept, decide the goal and permitted conditions and write them in the

101
00:05:19,310 --> 00:05:19,913
same structure.

102
00:05:20,100 --> 00:05:24,680
A pictured surface where a word and lighting change, matching colored balls, and

103
00:05:24,680 --> 00:05:26,340
combat are different actions.

104
00:05:26,640 --> 00:05:29,665
Do not automatically treat the next edited shot as the result of the previous

105
00:05:29,665 --> 00:05:29,940
action.

106
00:05:30,280 --> 00:05:36,983
Pepper’s water vehicle also appears in separate shots: traveling over flat water and

107
00:05:36,983 --> 00:05:38,247
rising over waves.

108
00:05:38,587 --> 00:05:41,907
This edit alone does not establish one continuous chase.

109
00:05:42,182 --> 00:05:46,028
Shooting on the cylinder’s picture includes ground movement and airborne attacks in

110
00:05:46,028 --> 00:05:46,862
separate sections.

111
00:05:47,362 --> 00:05:50,333
Here we observe each movement and the approaching targets.

112
00:05:50,333 --> 00:05:53,144
Some footage comes from an official presentation of assistance features.

113
00:05:53,484 --> 00:05:56,931
We do not turn that presentation into a claim about default difficulty or how every

114
00:05:56,931 --> 00:05:57,804
entry point is shown.

115
00:05:58,010 --> 00:06:01,141
As you read the sentences for your new concept, separate what the footage

116
00:06:01,141 --> 00:06:02,770
establishes from what you will decide.

117
00:06:02,970 --> 00:06:05,590
Do not let the reference make those promises for you.

118
00:06:10,233 --> 00:06:14,513
Ask the listener to describe, in their own words, what they would do first.

119
00:06:14,753 --> 00:06:18,194
The question checks the action you conveyed rather than asking them to identify a

120
00:06:18,194 --> 00:06:18,873
reference title.

121
00:06:19,313 --> 00:06:23,077
If a hypothetical answer is entering any wall, check whether the condition about

122
00:06:23,077 --> 00:06:24,253
entry points was missing.

123
00:06:24,433 --> 00:06:27,713
Restatement is a checking method we are proposing.

124
00:06:28,013 --> 00:06:31,748
Check whether the goal and actions were understood as intended, identify undecided

125
00:06:31,748 --> 00:06:33,933
conditions, and clarify the necessary sentences.

126
00:06:34,133 --> 00:06:37,253
The listener’s questions show where the concept needs more detail.

127
00:06:37,283 --> 00:06:40,703
On the illustrated surface, the character strikes enemies and moves along the route.

128
00:06:40,923 --> 00:06:45,283
Do not bundle entry movement and the combat after entry into one action.

129
00:06:45,563 --> 00:06:49,303
A later red chest opens and a card graphic appears.

130
00:06:49,643 --> 00:06:54,351
The earlier movement of the key does not justify promising that every chest opens or

131
00:06:54,351 --> 00:06:55,303
that combat ends.

132
00:06:55,543 --> 00:06:59,303
Check whether a listener can separate these differences in their own words.

133
00:06:59,363 --> 00:07:02,740
Distinguishing the visible result from a connection condition still to be decided

134
00:07:02,740 --> 00:07:03,823
reveals missing questions.

135
00:07:03,867 --> 00:07:07,578
On the illustrated page, the character approaches enemies, fights and moves to

136
00:07:07,578 --> 00:07:08,387
another platform.

137
00:07:08,627 --> 00:07:12,190
Separating the bright path from the dark one also brings out the setting hidden by

138
00:07:12,190 --> 00:07:13,147
the single word fight.

139
00:07:16,756 --> 00:07:20,236
On the desk, the character walks along a route or attacks near objects.

140
00:07:20,536 --> 00:07:24,748
Treat the exit from the illustrated page and the movement on the desk as separate

141
00:07:24,748 --> 00:07:30,780
shots, and you can describe where each action happens.

142
00:07:30,980 --> 00:07:34,651
Pepper’s movement out near water and above lava is different from firing toward an

143
00:07:34,651 --> 00:07:34,920
enemy.

144
00:07:35,160 --> 00:07:37,140
Describe the movement and its target together.

145
00:07:37,540 --> 00:07:40,466
In the mine, the character moves to avoid an approaching enemy and attacks.

146
00:07:40,486 --> 00:07:44,146
These actions do not establish every condition of combat.

147
00:07:45,533 --> 00:07:48,633
Read these last scenes as action verbs too.

148
00:07:49,273 --> 00:07:52,811
On pictured surfaces, the character fights and climbs stairs; on the desk, the

149
00:07:52,811 --> 00:07:55,033
character follows a route or rises with a rocket.

150
00:08:01,033 --> 00:08:04,393
Pepper launches from cannons and enters a large device.

151
00:08:04,633 --> 00:08:07,473
In other shots, the device moves and fires.

152
00:08:11,590 --> 00:08:15,070
These scenes do not establish the entry cost or a victory condition.

153
00:08:15,725 --> 00:08:19,485
When the action changes, reconsider the goal and conditions you need to explain.

154
00:08:19,765 --> 00:08:23,725
Recalling one familiar title does not by itself communicate these differences.

155
00:08:27,287 --> 00:08:30,868
Write your own new concept as three sentences: the goal, the action and its

156
00:08:30,868 --> 00:08:33,207
conditions, and the connection to another action.

157
00:08:33,487 --> 00:08:36,427
Even if you add a comparison, explain the central actions directly.

158
00:08:37,027 --> 00:08:40,307
Then use the listener’s restatement to find missing conditions.

159
00:08:40,787 --> 00:08:44,072
Check whether the same actions were understood, beyond whether both people know the

160
00:08:44,072 --> 00:08:44,507
same title.
